reduces down to more than three

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.880
actual instance that
need to be investigated,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.020
potentially remediated.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.210
At Google, it's very
important, and we

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.330
think it's very important,
to think like the attacker.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.540
So the attackers have a
particular kill chain.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.450
It's part of Google
culture to do red teaming

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.980
on a regular basis.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.930
And it was also part
of the informal culture

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.980
to do quite a bit of
white hat hacking.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.230
These things allow us
to really focus and hone

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.950
in on what the attackers
are actually focused in on.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.720
And so in thinking
like the attacker,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.390
the attackers are
focused really overall

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:38.550
in trying to target
identities, the code, the data,

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.260
the metadata, and the resources.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.440
On the identities, the typical
things that get compromised

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.900
are user accounts or off tokens.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.270
On the code side, the
injection of malware,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.270
they actually intersection
with even in the changing

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.635
the binaries, introducing things
into packages or libraries.

00:01:55.635 --> 00:01:57.510
On the data side, just
the ability to kind of

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.400
egress sensitive
data so they can use

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.965
that for a range of purposes.

00:02:00.965 --> 00:02:02.340
And on the metadata,
they'll look

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.570
for the attack on
cryptographic keys and secrets.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.460
And finally from the
resource standpoint,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.440
the really looking
at attacking things

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.510
from either denial
of service spaces

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:16.530
or subverting and abusing
resources for the purposes

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.260
of creating a command
control infrastructure,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.940
or a botnets, or
cryptocurrency mining.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.480
So all these are really kind
of where the state of play

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.730
is today.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.640
Now at Google, we've been
very fortunate to have

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.460
eight of our services reach
out to over a billion users,

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.310
including Google Cloud.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.010
As a result, every
moment of every day

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.710
we see the full range of attacks
that actually are going on,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.710
and the full range
of bad actors.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.610
So we see anything from lone
wolves and script kiddies

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.190
to nation state
actors to hacktivist

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.460
groups trying to attempt
to do the range of attacks

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:59.190
of phishing, denial of
service, malware injections,

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.500
for the full range
of motivations.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.430
Could be economic motivations,
espionage motivations,

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.650
or just purely for fame.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.630
As a result of that,
we've had to evolve

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:12.660
the culture and the strategy
for building security around

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.350
on Google, based on the global
scale in view of bad actors we

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.250
get while maintaining the pace
that supports the innovation

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.549
that the core products
within Google need.

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:24.090
As a result of that
experience, we've

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.910
learned a few lessons
of how to secure Google

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.380
and how to secure
Google customers

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.000
and enable them to
secure themselves

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.120
that we'd like to share.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.220
So there are about seven
kind of key highlights

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.270
that we want to
talk about today.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.860
And the first of those is
really defense in depth

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.960
by default at scale.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.240
And that's protecting again
those three main issues

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.320
we talked about.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.380
The identities, the data
in its three various forms,

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.670
code, user data, and
metadata, and then

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.830
the underlying resources.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.630
And then trust
through transparency.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:01.680
So not only is it necessary
and important to always

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.170
have leading edge
technology, in order

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:08.521
to build the trust with us
as customers and as partners,

00:04:08.521 --> 00:04:10.020
we actually need
to be more and more

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.250
transparent about the security
infrastructure that we have.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.709
Automation of best practices.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:22.470
So on the development side, the
ability to take the user out

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.680
of the decision
making process, that

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.810
take developers out of
bespoke creating things,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.310
to actually automate
the practices

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.490
so they get the best and most
secure things by default.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:36.270
And then similarly. a core
part of Google culture

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.190
is being open and
sharing innovation,

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.710
and that's a core part
of the security culture.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.620
We very much believe that
a safer for Web for all

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:48.060
is beneficial for the
masses and for everyone.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.340
And our ability to take the
number of our innovations

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.310
and either open source
them, open standard them,

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.750
or make them available to the
public increases and raises,

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:00.100
is the tie that
raises a lot of votes.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.010
On the user side, it's
important to address,

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:06.110
programmatically,
common use cases.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.950
So again, that we take the user
out of the decision process,

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.200
and that common things
like patches and updates

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.030
occur programmatically
and by default. Finally,

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.630
from a security standpoint
in a public cloud in customer

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:21.250
environment, its
important for us

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.010
to first be clear about where
the shared responsibility

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.440
line is, and really to
empower our customers

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:29.530
to deliver on their part
of that responsibility.

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:32.260
And lastly, with all of these
things in combination, trust

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.636
and security you can really
be an accelerant to business.

00:05:34.636 --> 00:05:37.051
And so now I'm going to talk
about a bunch of these things

00:05:37.051 --> 00:05:37.970
in a bit more detail.

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:51.370
So historically, users
customers and partners

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:55.210
have really depended on the
network as that key barrier

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.250
for security.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:00.010
And they really had
an intellectual model

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:03.370
of the villains being
kept outside and then

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:07.510
all the targets and things they
want to protect be kept inside.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.770
But that model is broken.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.180
Today, if either someone
gets inside or someone

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.240
starts inside the wire,
there are fundamentally

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.900
far fewer obstacles, controls,
and defenses for them.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:23.800
And enterprises are seeing
that in their day to day life.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.010
As enterprises now perforate
their own perimeters,

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.830
to use cloud services,
to allow mobile access,

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:33.250
this kind of core
perimeter model is broken.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:35.470
Now Google for
quite a while now,

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:37.250
we've not had a
perimeter based model.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.940
We do not believe that
there is an abstraction that

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:42.730
allows you to say there's
an outside and an inside.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:45.970
In fact, our solution is
a perimeter-less model,

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:49.690
based on the fact that we can
do identity and authentication

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:53.380
and policy on each
access to every resource.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:56.440
And we've talked about that
in our BeyondCorp papers

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:59.770
that we've published over
the last three to four years.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:03.640
A key part of that is that many
of the services Google delivers

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.610
are public to the
public web by nature.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:09.610
So in order to keep a
security level high enough

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.070
to protect the users
using those services,

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.140
we've had to build
a defense in depth

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.380
at scale by default structure.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:20.540
And we'll talk about it
infrastructure in a moment.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:21.970
So the first lesson.

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:26.470
Integrate the defense in depth
at scale by default. At Google

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.100
when we think about the
defense of our infrastructure,

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.870
we really think about
map to nine layers.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.500
From hardware,
through secure boot,

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.320
to inner process communications,
and the curated operating

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.440
system, to the storage
and data services,

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.490
the network that Eric and Diane
talked about earlier today,

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:48.930
application level security,
deployment, operations,

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.370
and old data usage.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.640
So at each layer of
these, we've designed

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.730
so that we can have
provenance and minimal trust

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:00.760
between each layer, which
allows us to then know

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:04.210
that as an application spins
up or as the hardware spins up,

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.020
it's built on an increasingly
trusted and known platform,

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.580
so that when it
finally operates,

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:14.050
it's operating in the situation
where the right code is

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:17.710
authorized by the right service,
running on the right machine,

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:20.710
accessing the right
data at the right time,

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:22.270
and in the right context.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:24.040
That's a core part
of every operation

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.390
that occurs at Google.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.240
To dive us in a
little bit more depth,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.940
we're going to talk about
many of the layers that

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:33.720
form the underlying layer,
so that the hardware level--

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:36.460
and we're very fortunate that
we have a large enough scale

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.620
that we can build
purpose built hardware.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:42.309
And that means from purpose
built chips going into purpose

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:45.755
built servers, into purpose
built storage, into personal

00:08:45.755 --> 00:08:49.810
built networking, into purpose
built and wholly owned data

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.900
centers that we
operate globally.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:54.700
After we have the
provenance therefore

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.170
in the supply chain
of those servers,

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:59.810
the next step is to boot
in a trusted fashion.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:03.490
So we cryptographically sign
the boot, the boot loader, all

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.320
the way up to the basic kernel.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.390
And therefore allows us
to now have a machine

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:09.880
state, which is in
a known good state

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.000
with a unique
cryptographic identity that

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.600
then allows us to determine
that it's ready to be joined

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.130
to the fleet.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.500
Once it's kind of at
that point, we also

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:23.080
have our own curated
and hardened version

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:26.110
of KVM, which we
spent a lot of time

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:30.100
through both code analysis,
fuzzing, and actually

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:32.260
changes and
modifications the code

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.510
to make sure that
that's a secure core

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:37.690
basis for our hardware and
software infrastructure.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:39.730
We have authentication
and cryptographic way

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.160
between services
at this low level.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.092
And then we want a
curated image of Unix,

00:09:45.092 --> 00:09:46.800
again that's been
stripped out, oh sorry,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.120
of Linux that's been stripped
out of many of the known

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:50.170
vulnerabilities.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.510
In all these cases of known
vulnerabilities we've found,

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.120
we've also published back
out to the security community

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.649
so that the world at large
can benefit from this.

00:09:58.649 --> 00:10:00.940
So now we're at a point where
we have trusted hardware,

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:03.790
it's boosted in trusted state,
and underlying host software

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:05.830
is something we have
high confidence in.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:10.780
At a storage level, we encrypt
everything at risk by default,

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:12.550
in one or more different ways.

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:14.906
We have underlying,
obviously, disk encryption,

00:10:14.906 --> 00:10:16.780
hardware disk encryption,
but on top of that,

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:20.620
add an application layer for all
data accesses on Google cloud

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.240
were encrypted at rest.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.820
We additionally encrypt
in-transit between data

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.200
centers we own.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.290
And we also allow that
ability to control the access

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:32.410
and logging of the data,
so through our set of audit

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:36.310
logs and also through
set of internal logging.

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:38.930
At the network layer, as both
Eric and Diane pointed out

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:40.930
this morning, we have one
of the world's largest

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:42.252
private networks.

00:10:42.252 --> 00:10:43.960
And what that means
for customers is they

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.210
can connect with us at over 100
different points of presence

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:48.890
across 33 countries.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:53.660
And from that point forward,
they're on a private network,

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:55.130
completely off the
public internet.

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:57.050
And we can guarantee
that end to end,

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.420
from where they touch
the point of presence

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:01.670
to the actual servers
and systems that

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:05.220
serve their applications.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.070
In addition, once you
have the network in place,

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.330
next piece is really
the applications,

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:11.940
the applications that we build.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:14.850
So within Google, we
have a software lifecycle

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:19.020
that programmatically enforces
peer review, security review,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.900
privacy review, before
one line of code

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:23.700
is pushed to production.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.270
And in addition, we use static
code analysis on our source

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:31.530
code, combined with the
ability to look at and scan

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.410
the libraries and the
packages that are linked in,

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.510
so that throughout
the build cycle,

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.820
in the manifest we have
a chain of evidence

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.070
and a binary signature
that allows us to know

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:43.290
that our code that's
about to be deployed

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:44.750
is actually the right code.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:46.410
And this gets back
into I said earlier

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.300
about making sure
the right code is

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.920
running on the right machine,
which has an identity.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:54.600
Authorized by the right service
or user who has an identity,

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:56.730
with those right
permissions, accessing

00:11:56.730 --> 00:12:01.417
the right data, at the right
time, in the right context.

00:12:01.417 --> 00:12:03.750
Once we're through with, from
an application development

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:07.020
standpoint, through the
software provenance,

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.540
through the ability to actually
actively scan that software,

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.810
the next step is to deploy.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:14.580
And at Google, we have a
phrase that your first users

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:16.380
are typically abusers.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:18.180
So there's always
the opportunity,

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.150
as soon as you
deploy code, to see

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.300
from a different set of
ways, the attackers trying

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:27.600
to find ways to actually
breach the actual software

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.010
you're creating.

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:31.770
And there are a number of things
we do on the deployment side.

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.870
First, for our
users, we enable them

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.120
to have a hardware
phishing-resistant two factor

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:37.920
authentication.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.280
And we enforce that all of
our employees with access

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.700
to anything at Google use
that same hardware to factor

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:47.610
phishing-resistant security key.

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:51.255
In addition, all Google services
are encrypted through TNS, end

00:12:51.255 --> 00:12:53.280
to end.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.850
Google as an overall
infrastructure

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.730
has a Google front-end,
which is globally

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:02.460
distributed at different
points of presence,

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:04.340
but logically centralized.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:06.120
And that gives us
the ability also to,

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:09.786
by default, have built in now
a service DDoS protection,

00:13:09.786 --> 00:13:11.160
for a number of
things, like SSYN

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.530
flood, a lot of the
volumetric metric attacks.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:15.690
And as a cloud
customer, if you use

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:17.880
one of the load
balancers, a product,

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:19.860
you're also protected
behind that.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.500
As well as if you use
our platform of services,

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:25.770
such as AppEngine or
BigQuery, or software services

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:27.480
like G Suite, those
are automatically

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:30.960
protected from DDoS attacks.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.540
In addition, we've
invested further

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:37.230
in the actual encrypting
and certification of traffic

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:39.810
between points through
certificate pinning, which

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:42.810
prevents man in
the middle attacks,

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.120
as well as moving forward
the work that we're

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.180
doing in an open way around
certificate authorities, which

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:49.890
allows you to have
an open record

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:52.620
and make sure that if a
particular CA gets compromised,

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.410
that that won't then be
able to be injected in

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:58.000
and create another
vector for folks.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.870
So once things are deployed,
now we have to operate them.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.570
And one of the core parts of
Google and our end of structure

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:06.240
is that we have the underlying
infrastructure from the host

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:09.160
side automatically
patched and updated.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:10.890
And this is something
that customers

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:13.560
don't have to provide
maintenance windows

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:17.310
or see downtime for us to do
if they opt our live migration.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:21.390
Because everything that Google
runs on a uniform substrate,

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:24.330
we're able to live migrate
user information around

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:26.250
while we're making either
maintenance changes

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:28.374
or importantly, these
patches and updates that make

00:14:28.374 --> 00:14:31.320
sure everything stays current.

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.390
In addition from an
operations standpoint,

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:35.890
we have our own
threat analysis team,

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:37.860
which focuses on the
type of attackers

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.440
that we see targeting Google.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.460
The advanced persistent
threats, looking

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:44.310
at the full range of
data we have at Google,

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.770
and look at the signatures
and the modes that they use,

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:49.200
so that we can better protect
ourselves and our customers

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.660
and our customers data.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:57.300
And we have both a pre-fail team
focused on security engineering

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.240
and proactively and
preventatively securing

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:03.000
our applications, as well as
a post-fail team and instant

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.060
response, focused on actually
investigating and taking

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.530
those millions of
events and turning those

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:10.890
into deciding whether
those are instances that

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:13.050
need to be explored or not.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:18.270
Finally from a usage standpoint,
as the applications are used,

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:19.890
we have the ability
through, again,

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.370
the data that we collect,
to look at login reputation,

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:26.910
to look at URL reputation,
in-app reputation,

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.150
things like Android store,
so we get a full sense

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.450
of what actually is
potentially a good binary

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:37.110
versus bad binary, what's good
behavior versus bad behavior.

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:39.150
And from a Google
Cloud standpoint,

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:43.230
we provide audit logging for
both access logs as well as

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:45.510
admin logs, so that
you as a customer

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:48.640
can see what's also going on.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:52.560
So as a part of this
then, in addition to

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:54.870
the technology that
we've put in here,

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.450
we've also continue to
invest in the people.

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:00.840
So we have 700 plus software
engineers and security

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:04.380
engineers solely focused on
securing Google and Google

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:06.030
customers on Cloud.

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:09.480
And then it's the combination
really of the technology

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:12.090
and the team that continues to
allow us to deliver security

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.070
at a high level standard.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:15.930
In addition, if you
want to learn more

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.360
about our infrastructure
security design,

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:21.090
I highly recommend that you
attend Niels Provos's talk

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.300
tomorrow at 5:30.

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:26.730
It's session IO243 and
he'll go in even more

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:29.260
detail around our
infrastructure security.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:32.040
So again, the first lesson
from securing Google and Google

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:35.250
customers is it's important
to have integrated defense

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:37.590
and depth that's scaled
by default, which

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:39.120
means that from an
end to end basis,

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.370
you can establish provenance
and you know exactly what

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:42.960
code you're running,
and you know what

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:46.240
your known secure state is.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.300
So lesson two is, in
addition to the technology

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:51.040
we just talked about,
and that's core,

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:52.450
right, it's a
necessary condition

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:55.120
to have leading technology
from a security standpoint.

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:58.720
For you as a customer and
you as partners to trust us,

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:01.510
we believe highly in trust
through transparency.

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:04.869
And what that means is that
it's a part of Google security

00:17:04.869 --> 00:17:07.700
culture, that whenever
possible, we allow you

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:10.720
as the customer and the
user to decide for yourself

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:12.920
and have visibility
in what we're doing,

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:15.880
so you can make decisions
and draw your own conclusions

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.890
about how we're running
things and whether it's

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:19.690
appropriate to your business.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:23.109
And some examples of this
are our transparency report,

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:25.930
which gives, on a regular
basis, a view of how

00:17:25.930 --> 00:17:30.610
legal and public actions
affect sensitivity and access

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:33.530
to information on the internet.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:39.260
In addition, we have an
ongoing and recurring effort

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:42.950
for increasing certifications,
so that once again, you

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:44.930
can either inspect
yourself or you

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:48.530
can trust and invest in a
third party that has already

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:49.850
validated what we do.

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:57.880
This spans the full range of ISO
27 001 27 0 17 and 18, SOC 1,

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:01.250
2, and 3, FedRAMP for a
number of our services,

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:05.570
especially G Suite, the ability
to do model contract clauses

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:08.660
with customers, and the
ability also assigned the case

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:10.569
for HIPAA for a number
of our services.

00:18:10.569 --> 00:18:12.110
And this is an area
that we are going

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:15.837
to continually to invest in
and increasingly invest in

00:18:15.837 --> 00:18:17.420
for the additional
certifications that

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:19.910
are prioritized by our
customers matched to what

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:21.590
they need for their business.

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:23.900
And also from a trust
and transparency side,

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:25.970
another piece of
how this is aligned

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:30.140
is to the SAP announcement that
we made this morning as well.

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:33.360
Partnering with SFP to deliver
governance, risk management,

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.210
and compliance
solutions, and also

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:38.810
partnering with SAPE so the
SAP can become a data custodian

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:39.970
of data on Google Cloud.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:45.850
The third lesson is
really around automating

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:48.650
best practices to prevent
common mistakes at scale.

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:51.220
So as I mentioned
earlier, us as humans

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:54.880
are the source of many of the
potential issues and problems.

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:57.850
And so from a development side,
it's very important for us

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:03.100
to automate as much as possible
and abstract out the decisions

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:06.040
and choices our developers
have to make that may lead them

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:09.230
to create a less secure state.

00:19:09.230 --> 00:19:11.310
As I also mentioned,
for most of our services

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:15.530
that we deploy in the public are
at the web application level,

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:17.750
so at the L seven level.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:21.950
So a common area of concern
and prioritization for us

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:23.840
is in cross-site scripting.

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:26.990
So as an example of some of
the automation that we've done,

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:30.170
we have frameworks and we
have secure coding practices.

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:32.780
Combine those with
automated scanning.

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.060
And what that's allowed
us over a four year period

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:38.300
is reduce cross-site
scripting errors by over 30%.

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:40.749
And for some of our
billion user applications,

00:19:40.749 --> 00:19:43.040
that's reduce cross-site
scripting vulnerabilities down

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:43.680
to near zero.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:49.200
The fourth lesson,
and this is again core

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.710
to Google, both from an
innovation open-source

00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:55.010
standpoint, is really
to share innovations

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:57.350
to the wider community
and really raise the bar

00:19:57.350 --> 00:19:59.060
and lift the tide,
so that the web

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:03.050
becomes more secure for all.

00:20:03.050 --> 00:20:06.380
Some examples of this,
Google has for a long time

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:08.630
been a contributor to
security and a leader

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:10.700
in the contributions
to security,

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:14.630
going back to TLS and the
Safe Browsing interface.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.440
Our vulnerability
rewards program,

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:19.460
which started back
in November of 2010,

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:22.000
has today paid out
over $9 million

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:25.070
to security researchers, who
have found various things

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:29.210
and helped us improve the
security of our own platform.

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:31.840
The security key technology
that I talked about,

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:35.020
in combination with
the FIDO Alliance,

00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:38.470
to again provide a phishing
resistant way that combines,

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:42.050
from a user experience, low
effort, and a high security

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:45.980
ability, so that actually will
be used and secure our users

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.080
and employees.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.510
Project Zero, which is
an independent effort

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:53.870
within Google, which looks for
O days and third party software,

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:56.000
and has been instrumental
in driving fixes

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:57.470
to some of the key
vulnerabilities

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:59.120
that have been found.

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:03.380
And recently over
the last year, we've

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:06.020
actually open sourced our
vendor security framework

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:08.360
to allow you to
actually take advantage

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.100
of some of the controls,
some of the ways

00:21:10.100 --> 00:21:13.690
we think about things when we
engage with vendors at Google,

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:16.250
to actually allow access
to our Chrome fuzzer

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:18.710
so that you can use the
infrastructure from a fuzzing

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:22.220
standpoint, and also from
an IoT beacon standpoint,

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:24.860
to extend from an open
format standpoint,

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:28.100
a secure and privacy
preserving beacon format.

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.930
And the one thing
also I mentioned back

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:32.630
in for our work in
KVM, each of the bugs

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:35.870
that we found in the KVM,
we've actually, again,

00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:38.660
externalized and made
available to the security

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:40.640
community, so they
can benefit from that.

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:42.800
And I think to date
that's been, we've

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:46.164
found about over 2/3 the
actual publicized bugs

00:21:46.164 --> 00:21:47.330
that have been found in KVM.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:54.170
From a fifth lesson
standpoint, it's

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:56.360
also from an
operational standpoint,

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:59.480
to address common use
cases programmatically.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:00.980
And some of the key
examples of this

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:04.790
is that while there's a lot of
press today around zero days

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:07.520
and around very sophisticated
advanced persistent threats,

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:09.530
most of our customers
and most partners

00:22:09.530 --> 00:22:13.400
still have a lot of potentially
basic hygiene security

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:17.060
things that can be addressed,
such as patching and updating

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:19.950
and what users do to
secure themselves.

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:23.300
An example of our philosophy
around updates and patching

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:26.690
is, this is the last
three versions of Chrome

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:30.440
that have been released
over since September.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:34.550
And basically what you see on
the x-axis is the week in which

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:37.970
the week by week of the
year, and the y-axis,

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:39.500
the percentage of adoption.

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:45.260
So within the first week of each
of the launches of our updates,

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:47.390
the majority of the
1 billion plus users

00:22:47.390 --> 00:22:49.220
are updated to the new version.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:51.890
And that's a core part of
both our Chrome and Android

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:54.020
approach, and also our
approach to patching

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:56.600
in our underlying fleet, is
that it should be an automated

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:58.790
process, it should
happen by default,

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:01.970
and therefore people get
to be secure by default

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:04.140
without having to take any
additional effort piece.

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:09.350
The next piece is focused,
again around users.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.810
As I mentioned before
on the development side,

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:13.940
us as humans are some
of the weakest links,

00:23:13.940 --> 00:23:16.160
and similarly on the user side.

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:21.650
Users and phishing is the most
common way that breaches start.

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:24.170
And again, talking to,
one of the key solutions

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:26.720
that we helped bring
to market is really

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:28.986
the phishing-resistant
hardware two factor.

00:23:28.986 --> 00:23:30.360
You'll be hearing
more about that

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.060
in the rest of this conference.

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:35.210
And that's something that,
again, as employees of Google,

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:36.150
it's required.

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:38.130
It's how we access
and secure ourselves

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:39.820
to any access at
Google, and it's

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:42.800
something we highly recommend
that the admin accounts

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:46.610
at any of the, and if you as
users or anyone on Google Cloud

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:51.350
also use this phishing-resistant
two factor solution.

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:53.960
In addition, you'll
see most of us

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:55.820
at Google also use Chromebooks.

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:59.330
And Chromebooks are another
way that, by default, you

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:00.290
can be secure.

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.630
Chromebooks, there is no
way to download software

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:03.800
onto Chromebooks.

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:07.010
So if you think about
hardware resistant two factor,

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:09.890
the ability to eliminate, the
ability to install malware

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:12.860
on your machines, those
are ways, by default

00:24:12.860 --> 00:24:16.040
again, that the community in
general can be more secure

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:20.700
and eliminate one of the
easiest attack vectors.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:24.060
So lesson 6 really is
around empowering customers

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.560
to fulfill their responsibility
in the shared responsibility

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:28.950
model for security.

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:30.644
So for most of you
in the audience,

00:24:30.644 --> 00:24:32.310
you're probably very
familiar with this.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:34.130
This is the 2011
Lockheed Martin kill

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:36.450
chain for attackers,
which really

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:39.510
looks to provide how
the attackers think

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:42.270
about ultimately getting
to their objectives.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:44.910
But to date, there hasn't
been a similar common view

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:47.520
of what the defender
kill chain looks like.

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:50.670
Some of the initial work on
that has really focused really

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:52.110
on post-fail, right.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.380
Really on what happens between
the monitoring and detecting,

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:59.010
the ability to alert, and
then the actions thereafter.

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:01.350
If you break that
up some more looking

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:04.660
at just the post-fail
piece, work recently

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:07.170
at RSA from
Microsoft has further

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:09.660
expanded some of
the response choices

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:12.510
and giving us more process
and more framework around what

00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:14.910
folks do on a response basis.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.400
And if you look at the original
work from Lockheed Martin,

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:20.130
that also really focused
just on the response side.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:22.190
So what we can do
to the attacker

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:25.560
killed chain, to either detect
it, deny it, destroy it,

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:27.090
deceive it.

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:29.730
But traditionally,
most of these things

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:32.640
have come from the
old world, where

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:35.290
a company has full control
of its data centers

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:36.540
and defensive structure.

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:39.390
And really, there's
only one organization

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:41.820
that's involved in
handling any incident.

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:44.700
As we've now moved to
kind of public cloud

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:46.860
and a cloud based world,
there are two things that

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:48.940
additionally need to happen.

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:52.260
One is increased, in
addition to that kill chain,

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:54.300
on the pre-fail
side, and having that

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:57.690
as an integrated part of the
overall decision on the defense

00:25:57.690 --> 00:25:58.440
side.

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:00.690
So in addition to the
post-fail reaction,

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:04.230
how do we, together
with our customers,

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:07.590
assess and provide the right
assessment the attack surface?

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:10.860
How do we actually specify,
define what the secure baseline

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:14.580
is, and have that enforced
across on premise, hybrid,

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:15.870
and multicloud?

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:17.954
And then how do we protect
those uniformly too,

00:26:17.954 --> 00:26:19.620
so there aren't any
chinks in the armor,

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:22.260
irrespective of what
the infrastructure is.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:25.490
And then that feeds into the
protection, detection alerting,

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:26.810
and response.

00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:30.780
And on the back end, one key
thing that historically, again,

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:33.180
has not been focused on, but
is more and more important,

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:35.220
is the communication aspect.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:37.440
Historically when we talked
about instant response

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:40.230
and detection response, it was
again within one organization

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:43.380
and so the sausage
making was really hidden.

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:45.360
At this point in
time, when customers

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:47.370
have a shared
responsibility on cloud,

00:26:47.370 --> 00:26:49.200
the distinction of the
communication paths

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:51.510
between the customer
and the cloud provider

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:54.570
need to both be
programmatically and processed

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:56.790
from a standpoint,
evolved so they actually

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.070
meet the needs on both sides.

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:01.670
And the last thing is really
the opportunity to learn.

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:04.860
So as both Diane and Fei-Fei
talked about this morning,

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:06.990
we're in the age of
machine learning.

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:09.090
So the ability to take
the data that we now

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:12.240
have across the cloud,
that you have as customers,

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:14.190
and use that to integrate
both in learning

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:16.590
on the pre-fail side,
in the detection

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:21.460
side, and the response side
is a great opportunity.

00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:24.380
So what we want to do now then
is connect this defender kill

00:27:24.380 --> 00:27:27.276
chain, this defender kill
chain, with those targets

00:27:27.276 --> 00:27:28.900
I talked about at
the beginning, right.

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:33.280
So attackers are looking to
get out, again, identity, code,

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:36.400
data, metadata, and resources.

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:38.860
When you combine this, you
can create a framework, which

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:41.590
really looks at the
intersection of the two,

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:43.900
both in pre-fail and
post-fail, and now we

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:47.140
can have a very, very clear
discussion between cloud

00:27:47.140 --> 00:27:48.940
providers and
customers about where

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:51.220
the shared responsibility sits.

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:53.340
So some of the key
places this sits.

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:55.570
If you think about where
audit and compliance sit,

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:59.070
they sit around in the pre-fail
assessment and specification

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:02.170
phase, that's what a lot of
audit and compliance is about.

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:03.610
In addition,
security engineering

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:07.930
sits in, similarly, how to
define from your development

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:10.910
cycle, the things that make
your code and your system

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:13.690
secure by design.

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:15.430
When we cross over to
the post-fail side,

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:17.860
that's really
historically been the role

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:21.490
of security operations, the
SOC, and the detection response

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:22.480
teams.

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:24.280
However one thing you'll
note purposefully,

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:27.550
is I haven't created a
overlap between those.

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:29.980
And that reflects what
life is like today,

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:31.540
but that's one of
the things that

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:34.080
needs to change as we
think about how to do end

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:38.800
to end security operations in,
similar to how folks ended up

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:41.170
doing development operations.

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:42.640
That overlap of
understanding what

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:45.646
you specify gets turned
into what you monitor for,

00:28:45.646 --> 00:28:47.020
gets turned into
what you detect,

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:49.360
and what you are able
to then respond to.

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:50.990
And as I talked
about before, there's

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:52.840
a huge opportunity
from the learning

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:57.340
side across all of these, to
feed into the growth of this.

00:28:57.340 --> 00:28:59.620
So let's take a test
drive of this in practice

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:01.550
and what does this mean.

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:05.800
So key intersections, again,
audit and compliance, security

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:08.519
around each of the
things, for example,

00:29:08.519 --> 00:29:10.060
security engineering
around identity,

00:29:10.060 --> 00:29:12.510
how does that become a
shared responsibility?

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:15.280
Around policy specification
and enforcement,

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:17.890
vulnerability management,
data protection,

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:20.122
logging, those are the
common topics that come up

00:29:20.122 --> 00:29:21.330
in the shared responsibility.

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:23.246
And from an operations
side, the communication

00:29:23.246 --> 00:29:24.910
and learning pieces.

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:28.950
Now when we map this to
identities, what we have here

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:33.640
then, just to orient you is,
right to left, on premise,

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:36.460
through to software as a
service, so the abstraction

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:39.310
the sense of control
for the customer

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:42.700
is going most on the
right, least on the left.

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:46.630
And then in the y-axis, the
stages of the kill chain, from

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:48.700
assess all the
way down to learn.

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:51.370
The color codes are what
the provider typically

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:55.060
manages in these situations,
versus on the green side, what

00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:56.470
the customer manages.

00:29:56.470 --> 00:29:59.230
And then the gold areas are
those opportunity areas,

00:29:59.230 --> 00:30:01.960
where even though this is
a customer responsibility

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:03.770
and customer managed
thing, theirs

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:06.250
is where either
providers or partners can

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:10.312
help the customer achieve
their shared responsibility.

00:30:10.312 --> 00:30:11.770
So what does this
mean in practice?

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:15.220
So from an identity
standpoint, assessing

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:17.095
the strength of your
identity and how

00:30:17.095 --> 00:30:18.970
your identity solutions,
that's squarely

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:21.430
on the customer, that's
not something that really

00:30:21.430 --> 00:30:23.020
cloud provider just decides.

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:27.280
But enabling the customer to
then understand and specify

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:28.960
what's the secure
baseline, those

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:30.940
are opportunities for
the cloud provider

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:33.280
and or partners to
help the customer

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:36.430
understand what their secure
state is, what the baseline is,

00:30:36.430 --> 00:30:38.560
and do the reference from that.

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:41.770
As you can tell, as you go
from a software as a service,

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:45.279
where very little is in the
direct control of the customer

00:30:45.279 --> 00:30:47.320
and the customer is really
using software created

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.720
by the provider,
there's a lot that's not

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:51.570
the customer's responsibility.

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:54.500
In the platform as a service
case is where that gray area,

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:57.280
there's a number of things in
the specify and protect side

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:00.400
that end up being the customer's
responsibility, as well

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:03.400
as that in all cases,
that opportunity to learn.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:06.605
And where most the folks doing
traditional lift and shift are,

00:31:06.605 --> 00:31:08.950
are obviously an
infrastructure of service.

00:31:08.950 --> 00:31:11.380
And throughout the spectrum,
besides just the assessment

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:14.770
side of identities,
the ability for us

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:18.880
as the cloud provider or our
partners to help customers

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:21.850
fulfill the responsibility
spans the whole spectrum.

00:31:21.850 --> 00:31:24.310
And including a responsibility
around the communication

00:31:24.310 --> 00:31:26.560
on the underlying
learning pieces.

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:31.330
And this kind of pattern is
largely the same from a data

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:32.260
standpoint.

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:34.930
The addition opportunity
from a data standpoint

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:38.170
is that cloud providers, and
we talked about this earlier

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:41.350
in terms of a content
awareness, have the ability

00:31:41.350 --> 00:31:44.380
to also assist customers
in their ability

00:31:44.380 --> 00:31:46.900
to understand what
data content they have,

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:50.420
what the policies there are,
and I'll talk more about that.

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:52.375
And finally from a
resource standpoint.

00:31:52.375 --> 00:31:54.250
So if you're talking
the underlying resources

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:55.300
to deliver services.

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:57.710
On a software as a
service basis for example,

00:31:57.710 --> 00:31:59.620
with something like
Drive or G-mail,

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:01.750
the customer really doesn't
have responsibilities

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:03.610
to protect those resources.

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:06.520
Those are auto-scaled resources
that the cloud provider

00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:07.420
is managing.

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:09.100
Similarly, in a
platform as a service,

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:11.520
these are operationally
things that the cloud provider

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:12.450
provides.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:14.200
Once you move to
infrastructure as service

00:32:14.200 --> 00:32:16.967
throughout the full spectrum,
as a cloud provider,

00:32:16.967 --> 00:32:18.550
and with our partners,
we have ability

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:21.640
to assist you as a
customer about how

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:24.365
you want to run and secure your
resource of infrastructures,

00:32:24.365 --> 00:32:25.990
whether it's against
denial of service,

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:30.540
whether it's against abuse,
and those type things.

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:33.510
So kind of the last lesson is to
take that shared responsibility

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:35.760
model and talk
about what does that

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:39.180
mean for creating a trusted,
underlying, infrastructure

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:40.950
for you as a customer.

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:43.350
And they're really focused
around three things.

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:47.250
Context aware access, so getting
back to what we talked about

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:50.490
within Google, we do the
right code is running

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:53.400
on the right machine,
authorized by the right user,

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:56.650
running at the right time,
and in the right context.

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:58.350
Being able to
externalize that for you

00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:01.200
as customers, to be
the same type of access

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:02.910
control that you guys provide.

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:05.520
From a content aware
standpoint, the ability

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:07.830
to not only look
at data and think

00:33:07.830 --> 00:33:09.630
about the encryption
of it, but also

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.430
think about the
classification of it

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:15.390
and also differentially both
specify and enforce policy

00:33:15.390 --> 00:33:17.250
against that data,
is an opportunity

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:19.140
that exists now in the
public cloud as data

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:20.370
gets moved to that.

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:23.190
That's much harder
for you guys to start

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:26.190
in practice in your existing
on prem infrastructures.

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:27.507
And lastly, platform trust.

00:33:27.507 --> 00:33:29.340
And we'll talk a little
bit more about where

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:33.640
we're thinking further on
the platform trust side.

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:36.700
So trust and
security historically

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:39.830
have been looked at as things
that slow down innovation.

00:33:39.830 --> 00:33:41.770
Our firm belief at
Google and something

00:33:41.770 --> 00:33:43.540
that we've lived
through, keeping up

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:45.790
with the velocity of
our production teams,

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:48.760
is that once you have the
right set of trust and security

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:51.700
by default, you actually
accelerate innovation,

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.410
because that allows
developers to focus

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:56.350
on their kind of core
practice, which is delivering

00:33:56.350 --> 00:33:59.027
and building great applications
that drive business,

00:33:59.027 --> 00:34:00.610
and that the underlying
infrastructure

00:34:00.610 --> 00:34:03.340
protects them and protects
their applications.

00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:05.800
And Eric talked about this
earlier in the keynote.

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:08.320
That is the model that
we use at Google, that's

00:34:08.320 --> 00:34:11.050
the model that we think
will, in the long term,

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:13.739
be the right one for
folks building businesses.

00:34:13.739 --> 00:34:17.440
In order to do that, the three
components that we see really,

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:19.989
are context aware secure access.

00:34:19.989 --> 00:34:22.929
So really extending beyond
this perimeter model

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:25.810
to allow individual
resources to have policy

00:34:25.810 --> 00:34:29.570
applied at the moment to
use against their identity.

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:31.159
Content aware data management.

00:34:31.159 --> 00:34:34.150
So as you all know,
the nature of the data,

00:34:34.150 --> 00:34:37.179
what's actually in the data,
typically dictates its risk.

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:40.870
So being able to make users
and customers more easily

00:34:40.870 --> 00:34:43.360
aware of what that content is
and manage that so they can

00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:45.219
match differential policies.

00:34:45.219 --> 00:34:46.900
And from a platform
trust standpoint,

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:48.820
the two kind of key principles.

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:50.800
One we talked
about before, which

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:52.810
is the trust through
transparency,

00:34:52.810 --> 00:34:55.630
the more we are able to show
you and have you validate

00:34:55.630 --> 00:34:57.502
what we do, the
higher level of trust

00:34:57.502 --> 00:34:58.960
that you'll have
the ability to run

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:01.900
your businesses as our
platform, and also enabling

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:03.580
minimal required trust.

00:35:03.580 --> 00:35:06.760
So how do we increase
the portfolio of items

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:09.670
that you can choose to do
without even having to trust

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:12.342
Google or the cloud provider.

00:35:12.342 --> 00:35:14.050
So these are the areas
that we're looking

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:15.133
at kind of moving forward.

00:35:17.550 --> 00:35:19.680
So kind of in summary.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:22.880
Defense in depth is
really around allowing

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:25.490
the infrastructure to
be secure by default

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:27.950
and then allowing users,
developers, clients,

00:35:27.950 --> 00:35:30.567
and partners to
really focus on what

00:35:30.567 --> 00:35:32.900
they do best, which is creating
what's next and building

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:34.300
businesses.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:36.320
The trust through
transparency piece

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:38.900
enables you to see and
verify for yourself,

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:40.975
and therefore make
decisions in an informed

00:35:40.975 --> 00:35:43.475
way about whether you want to
run certain business processes

00:35:43.475 --> 00:35:46.760
and workloads on
certain configurations.

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:50.840
Automation abstraction takes
humans out of the process

00:35:50.840 --> 00:35:55.450
and creates repeatability
and deliverability at scale.

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:59.140
Our core values around open
sourcing and sharing innovation

00:35:59.140 --> 00:36:01.600
are really around uplifting
the whole internet

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:04.600
and making it
safer for everyone.

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:06.710
And then from the
user standpoint,

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.560
to take again that
decision making choice out

00:36:09.560 --> 00:36:13.190
of things like updates,
patching, access,

00:36:13.190 --> 00:36:16.100
so that the common use cases
are addressed programmatically

00:36:16.100 --> 00:36:20.900
and folks are secure by
default. Empowering customers

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:22.010
to secure themselves.

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:25.820
So being clear about the shared
responsibility model, enabling

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:28.110
kind of a successful
shared responsibility

00:36:28.110 --> 00:36:30.470
by providing a full range
of services that allow

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:32.810
customers to deliver
on their part.

00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:36.200
And lastly, to accelerate
and continue to create you

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:38.262
security as an
accelerant, the ability

00:36:38.262 --> 00:36:41.990
to focus on context awareness,
content awareness, and then

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:43.880
platform trust.

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:47.900
So a core part of where we
are as an enterprise, working

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:52.721
with enterprises, is not only
to enable you to kind of get

00:36:52.721 --> 00:36:54.970
to where you want to go, but
to start with meeting you

00:36:54.970 --> 00:36:56.520
where you are, and
then taking you

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:58.370
beyond where you can imagine.

00:36:58.370 --> 00:37:02.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

